# Part 2: Planning

Plan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).

Canonical planning API:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)
task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model
task-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure
task-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees
```

Scope forms:

- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)
- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)
- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.

Compact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:

```
active:false
criticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04
elements:4
mode:children
scope:EPIC-01
statuses:{"TASK-01":"done","TASK-02":"development","TASK-03":"backlog","TASK-04":"backlog"}
waves:[["TASK-01","TASK-03"],["TASK-02"],["TASK-04"]]
```

- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel
- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain
- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)

The default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.

Local human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):

```bash
task-board plan                                    # full project plan
task-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan
task-board plan --save                             # save to file
```

Agents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.

